you  [Music]  this morning on the dish he helped  pioneer the development of California  cuisine then brought it thousands of  miles east to his hometown chef Zach  rule was born and raised in the  Cleveland area and while seemingly  destined to join a family business  became a chef instead he made his first  mark at the influential Michaels in  Santa Monica California and he thought  the blend of simple cooking techniques  and the freshest ingredients would be a  hit back home his first venues II  contemporary cuisine transformed the  local dining scene and led to his  current collection of eight Cleveland  area restaurants a cruel good morning  welcome to the dish  great pian here tell us about this  really great table okay here we go we've  got a cassoulet stuffed the oysters a  squash salad pommes frites  lemon polenta cake and we're gonna wash  it down with a barrel aged bourbon man  hat don't mind if I do may as well start  the morning off exactly know when you  got into cooking it was a little  different route you were actually in  business school and I would say an act  of defiance against your father got you  huh I was at the Wharton School of  Finance I was supposed to take over my  dad's business which was what he was a  manufacturer's rep I was the only Son  uh-huh resistors so I was being groomed  for that business and there was a kid  down the hall that had a hot a toaster  oven I had a hot plate and we just  started cooking that's how I spent my  freshman year at the University of  Pennsylvania and it had a dairy dad take  it when you said I'm not well I came  home after a semester and said I don't  think I want the business how did he  take it well he was sort of a guess and  then he said what are you gonna do say I  want to become a chef well that didn't  go over very well either back in 1972  nice upper middle-class Jewish kids from  Shaker Heights Ohio weren't becoming  chefs going to the family business or  lawyers did that stay with you as you  moved we talked about Michaels and then  obviously bring in cuisine back to  Cleveland did that always stay with you  of course I was chasing this ghost and  the ghost was my father and  and you know I wanted to prove to him  that I was gonna make it and I was gonna  succeed and that I was gonna do it at  the top level that I could yeah so your  drive was fired by that all along well  you know I probably am a Type A all  right but you know there was that  impetus behind me of course mm-hmm  did he ever have the moment of pride in  what you were doing oh yeah oh yeah I  mean unfortunately he didn't get to see  it but he knew before he died that it  was going to work he knew what I was  going to do yeah so that must have been  satisfying that that was great but you  know fortunately my mother lived to age  95 so she saw almost every restaurant  that I opened up you know Michael  Michaels in Santa Monica and the idea of  California cuisine back then when you  went there this was 1980 and you know  the the restaurant scene in the United  States wasn't it wasn't what it is now  so there were people flocking people  chefs that were flocking from all over  the world to Southern California because  there was a food revolution going on out  there all due respect to New York but  the food revolution at that point was  going out in Southern California and I  was fortunate at the age of 27 to be  right in the middle of it what made you  bring it back to Cleveland well I wanted  to settle up with my father yeah so I  had a two-year plan to go back there and  just stay there save all my money and  then go back to Southern California and  open up a restaurant in Santa Barbara  this was 1982 so I was supposed to be  believing in 1984 so I'm slightly behind  [Laughter]  well I realized that I had built up this  goodwill yeah and circumstances were  such that my dad got sick and I wasn't  just gonna leave well I'm gonna have you  sign our dish and as I do I'll ask you  if you could have this meal with anyone  pastor price  who would it be would be my father  definitely around the rock it would be a  round of golf and then dinner afterwards  yeah fantastic well chefs a cruel thank  you so very much for morons a cruel and  the dish you can head to our website at  CBS this morning calm

